<question type="multichoice">
  <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_EVOLUTION_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Natural selection curves stabilising directional disruptive</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
    <p>The graphs below show the operation of natural selection on a phenotypic trait starting from an initial bell-shaped distribution.</p>
    <p><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 560 320" width="560" height="320" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d7de;border-radius:6px;">
      <rect x="0" y="0" width="560" height="320" fill="#ffffff"/>
      <!-- Initial curve (left) -->
      <g transform="translate(20,105)">
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="165" height="115" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
        <path d="M 15 100 Q 50 98 82 22 Q 114 98 150 100 Z" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#991b1b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="82" y1="22" x2="82" y2="100" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,2"/>
        <text x="82" y="14" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">Initial Population</text>
        <line x1="10" y1="100" x2="155" y2="100" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="10" y1="100" x2="10" y2="18" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      </g>
      <!-- Arrows from initial -->
      <line x1="192" y1="130" x2="255" y2="62" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="3"/>
      <line x1="192" y1="162" x2="255" y2="162" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="3"/>
      <line x1="192" y1="195" x2="255" y2="262" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="3"/>
      <!-- Curve I: Directional -->
      <g transform="translate(265,12)">
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="220" height="92" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
        <path d="M 85 80 Q 118 78 145 20 Q 172 78 205 80 Z" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#991b1b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="145" y1="20" x2="145" y2="80" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,2"/>
        <text x="72" y="24" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">Peak shifts</text>
        <text x="72" y="36" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">in one direction</text>
        <text x="115" y="55" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">→</text>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="210" y2="80" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="15" y2="10" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="235" y="52" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">I</text>
      </g>
      <!-- Curve II: Disruptive -->
      <g transform="translate(265,114)">
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="220" height="92" fill="#cffafe" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
        <path d="M 25 80 Q 45 40 65 40 Q 88 40 110 66 Q 132 40 155 40 Q 175 40 195 80 Z" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#991b1b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="110" y="20" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">Two peaks form</text>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="210" y2="80" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="15" y2="10" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="235" y="52" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">II</text>
      </g>
      <!-- Curve III: Stabilising -->
      <g transform="translate(265,216)">
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="220" height="92" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
        <path d="M 55 80 Q 88 78 110 12 Q 132 78 165 80 Z" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#991b1b" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="110" y1="12" x2="110" y2="80" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,2"/>
        <text x="55" y="24" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">Peak gets higher</text>
        <text x="55" y="36" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111827">and narrower</text>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="210" y2="80" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="15" y1="80" x2="15" y2="10" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="235" y="52" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">III</text>
      </g>
    </svg></p>
    <p>Identify the types of natural selection represented by curves <strong>I</strong>, <strong>II</strong>, and <strong>III</strong> respectively.</p>
  ]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
    <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The diagram presents an initial bell-shaped phenotypic distribution curve on the left and three resulting distributions on the right: curve I shows the peak shifted in one direction, curve II shows two distinct peaks, and curve III shows a single peak that has become higher and narrower around the mean.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Biological Model</strong><br/>In each graph, the horizontal axis represents the phenotypic character value and the vertical axis represents the number of individuals possessing that phenotype. Natural selection alters the shape and position of the distribution based on which phenotypes have higher reproductive fitness.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Principles</strong><br/>Three modes of natural selection operate on quantitative traits: stabilising selection (favours the mean character value), directional selection (favours a character value other than the mean), and disruptive selection (favours peripheral character values at both ends of the distribution curve).</p>
    <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>- In curve I, more individuals acquire a value other than the mean character value, shifting the peak in one direction; this represents <strong>directional</strong> selection.<br/>- In curve II, more individuals acquire peripheral character values at both ends of the distribution curve, producing two peaks; this represents <strong>disruptive</strong> selection.<br/>- In curve III, more individuals acquire the mean character value, making the peak higher and narrower; this represents <strong>stabilising</strong> selection.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Matching each graph to its mode of selection gives: I = Directional, II = Disruptive, and III = Stabilising.</p>
  ]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <single>true</single>
  <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
  <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
  <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>I: Stabilising, II: Directional, III: Disruptive</p>]]></text></answer>
  <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>I: Disruptive, II: Stabilising, III: Directional</p>]]></text></answer>
  <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>I: Directional, II: Stabilising, III: Disruptive</p>]]></text></answer>
  <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>I: Directional, II: Disruptive, III: Stabilising</p>]]></text></answer>
