  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
    <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The schematic shows a mammalian forelimb skeleton from proximal (shoulder) to distal. Segment 1 is the single upper limb bone, segment 2 consists of the two parallel forearm bones, and segment 3 consists of the cluster of small wrist bones located immediately proximal to the metacarpals and phalanges.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Biological Model</strong><br/>Whales, bats, cheetahs, and humans are all mammals whose forelimbs perform very different functions (swimming, flying, running, and grasping, respectively) yet retain the same underlying skeletal blueprint.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Principles</strong><br/>When the same anatomical structure develops along different directions due to adaptations to different needs, the phenomenon is called <strong>divergent evolution</strong>, and the resulting structures are termed <strong>homologous organs</strong>, which indicate common ancestry.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>Tracing the mammalian forelimb bones from proximal to distal:<br/>- Label <strong>1</strong> is the single upper bone, the <strong>humerus</strong>.<br/>- Label <strong>2</strong> consists of the two forearm bones, the <strong>radius and ulna</strong>.<br/>- Label <strong>3</strong> consists of the wrist bones, the <strong>carpals</strong> (followed distally by metacarpals and phalanges).<br/>Because these anatomically similar forelimbs are modified for different functions in whales, bats, cheetahs, and humans, they represent homology and divergent evolution.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Therefore, 1 = Humerus, 2 = Radius and ulna, 3 = Carpals, and the shared pattern demonstrates divergent evolution (homology).</p>
  ]]></text></generalfeedback>
